BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Newburyport Art presents May Exhibitions at 65 Water Street
DTSTART:20261112T110000
DTEND:20261112T170000
DTSTAMP:20261110T190930Z
UID:f8bc6f41cfa59c3ff507e580b2b4ed@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:Newburyport Art (NAA) presents three new shows opening in the 
 month of May! Soft Structures is a group exhibition that explores fibers a
 nd fibrous material as process and metaphor. This exhibit features over 80
  artworks in a variety of media including drawing\, painting\, sculpture\,
  weaving\, embroidery\, quilting\, collage\, mixed media and more. This ex
 hibit is on view in the first-floor Sargent Gallery and second-floor Harts
 on Gallery. FOLIA is a collaborative exhibition featuring works by Amesbur
 y artists Amy Vander Els & Heather Karp. This exhibit explores the quiet b
 eauty and joyful rhythm of leaves and the symbolic meaning behind many tha
 t speaks of perseverance\, resilience and hope. Thirty-six artworks are di
 splayed amongst spring greenery in the first-floor Hills Gallery. Amy and 
 Heather will give a free artist talk on Thursday\, May 21st from 6:00-7:00
 pm\, concluding with Q&A. The 11th Annual Outdoor Sculpture Garden Exhibit
 ion debuted on May 1st\, and will remain on view year-round until April 20
 27. At nearly 3\,000 square feet\, the Range Lights Sculpture Garden resid
 es behind the historic NAA and is seen and visited by hundreds of people e
 very week. This year’s exhibition features 16 pieces by 10 local and reg
 ional artists. The participating artists include\; Jeffrey Briggs\, Lindle
 y Briggs\, Sarah Burns and Mike Platt\, Alexis Guieb-Alimbuyuguen\, Andrew
  Henschel\, Mae Kenny\, Evan Ralph\, Michael Updike and Raynor Warner. A r
 eception for FOLIA and Soft Structures will be held on Saturday\, May 2nd 
 from 4-6pm\, with the 11th Annual Sculpture Exhibit reception beginning at
  3:00pm\, including a Best in Show award at 3:30pm. The receptions are fre
 e and open to the public\, and visitors participating in the May 2nd Newbu
 ryport Artwalk are encouraged to stop by. New Hours: Wednesday through Sun
 day from 11:00am–5:00pm. The gallery is handicap accessible. More inform
 ation\, as well as an online gallery is available at newburyportart.org. F
 ounded in 1948\, Newburyport Art\, a 501(c)(3) non-profit\, is dedicated t
 o promoting and exhibiting members’ work and to providing art education 
 and access to the visual arts for the entire community. The gallery and ev
 ents are free and open to the public.\n\nSources: North of Boston\nEvent p
 age: https://northofboston.org/event/newburyport-art-presents-may-exhibiti
 ons-at-65-water-street/2026-11-12/
LOCATION:Newburyport Art Association\, 65 Water St\, Newburyport\, MA\, 01
 950
URL:https://northofboston.org/event/newburyport-art-presents-may-exhibitio
 ns-at-65-water-street/2026-11-12/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Open gallery hours: Small Works 2026 and Drinks on Me
DTSTART:20261112T000000
DTEND:20261213T000000
DTSTAMP:20261110T190930Z
UID:34fc77754ea9a520a8964895d5ec28@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:New exhibitions fill the ArtsWorcester galleries starting Nove
 mber 12\, and you won't want to miss all that's on view this holiday seaso
 n! In the East Gallery\, the annual favorite Small Works 2026 fills the sp
 ace with miniature works\, all measuring 10" x 10" or under. Artworks had 
 no medium or theme requirement\, making for a delightfully robust array of
  teeny treasures. Expect unique finds like an abstract turtle made from pl
 aster\, a miniature woven handbag\, a wooden bowl with the aura of a UFO\,
  and much more to be adored. Small works make unique holiday gifts\, too\,
  so you might find something perfect for someone in your life (or find som
 ething for your own personal collection)! In the West Gallery\, the solo s
 how Drinks on Me: interdisciplinary artist Amanda (Andi) Pickler explores 
 the spectrum of intimacy that exists within the domesticity of interperson
 al relationships\, and the public and private spaces of queer nightlife. P
 ickler's saturated\, jewel-toned paintings and detailed prints include sce
 nes from her trips to lesbian and queer bars around the country. These ima
 ges and the spaces they depict offer connection\, joy\, and respite to a c
 ommunity experiencing significant challenges and threats. Both exhibitions
  are on view starting November 12 through December 13 during gallery hours
 : Wednesdays through Sundays\, each day from 12pm to 5pm. On December 10\,
  ArtsWorcester will be open until 7 PM for late night hours. Gallery hours
  are always open and free to all. ArtsWorcester // 44 Portland Street\, Wo
 rcester\, MA 01608 On view November 12 - December 13 // Free and open to a
 ll // https://artsworcester.org/ Concurrently on view will be the Assets f
 or Artists 2026 Showcase in the third floor Davis Art Gallery. See our web
 site for more information on upcoming shows and events. Parking is availab
 le at the Worcester Public Library (McGrath) Lot\, Federal Plaza Garage\, 
 Worcester Common Garage\, and Pearl-Elm Garage. Metered street parking is 
 also available. On weekends\, and on weekdays after 5:00 PM\, visitors may
  park in the Printers Building parking lot free of charge. Please do not p
 ark in the lot across the street from the Printers Building\; you will be 
 immediately towed. // Photos: Andi Pickler\, They Don't Get To Tell Us Whe
 ther Or Not This Is Real\, oil on canvas\, 48" x 36"\, 2026 Andi Pickler\,
  Our Hands At Ginger's\, oil on panel\, 14" x 18"\, 2025 Sarah Beard\, It 
 Started to Clear Part Way Up\, 9" x 9"\, acrylic and collage\, 2025 Marjor
 ie Hart\, Hand woven purse with chain handle\, 8" x 8" x 0.5"\, 2024\n\nSo
 urces: Discover Central Massachusetts\nEvent page: https://www.discovercen
 tralma.org/event/open-gallery-hours%3a-small-works-2026-and-drinks-on-me/3
 1071/
LOCATION:ArtsWorcester\, 44 Portland St.\, Worcester\, MA\, 01608
URL:https://www.discovercentralma.org/event/open-gallery-hours%3a-small-wo
 rks-2026-and-drinks-on-me/31071/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Raise the Sails Benefit
DTSTART:20261112T000000
DTEND:20261112T000000
DTSTAMP:20261110T190930Z
UID:eb8dff381c0c731ab9225a1b10be20@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:Since 1946\, Community Boating has been part of Boston’s his
 tory. From our boathouse on the Charles River Esplanade\, generations of y
 oung people have learned to sail\, built confidence\, made friends\, and d
 eveloped a connection to the river and the natural environment. Today\, we
  continue that work by making sailing accessible to people of all ages and
  abilities. This year’s benefit shines a light on our youth and adaptive
  sailing programs\, which open the experience of sailing to young people a
 nd people with disabilities. Support from this event helps provide scholar
 ships\, adaptive equipment\, instruction\, and opportunities for sailors w
 ho might otherwise not have access to the water. Join members\, alumni\, f
 riends\, and supporters for an evening of passed hors d’oeuvres\, food s
 tations\, an open bar featuring beer\, wine\, and soft drinks\, music\, an
 d a silent auction at The Liberty Hotel. Help us celebrate 80 years of sai
 ling in Boston and support the next generation of sailors and our growing 
 community of adaptive sailors. LEARN MORE\n\nSources: Meet Boston\nEvent p
 age: https://www.meetboston.com/event/raise-the-sails-benefit/94006/
LOCATION:Liberty Hotel\, Liberty Hotel 215 Charles St\, Boston\, MA\, 0211
 4
URL:https://www.meetboston.com/event/raise-the-sails-benefit/94006/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Spotted Lanternfly Workshop
DTSTART:20261112T000000
DTEND:20261112T000000
DTSTAMP:20261110T190930Z
UID:0bba2df3c53f20e1f63d436662deaf@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:Location: Banx Room Audience: Adult Register here: mywpl.org/e
 vent/17704005 This illustrated program looks at lessons learned since the 
 spotted lanternfly was first discovered in Pennsylvania in 2014\, what sci
 ence is still working to understand\, and what Worcester residents can rea
 listically do as the insect becomes established here. The emphasis is on p
 ractical management not eradication. Joe Johnson is a Worcester resident a
 nd a commissioner on the city’s Urban Forestry Tree Commission. He volun
 teers with the Blackstone Heritage Corridor and several local conservation
  and park stewardship organizations. With training in arboriculture\, inva
 sive plant management\, and pollinator stewardship\, Joe helps residents u
 nderstand invasive species through fieldwork\, public presentations\, and 
 community education. His spotted lanternfly program emphasizes lessons lea
 rned in other states and realistic approaches to managing this widespread 
 insect. Want to stay in the know about all things WPL? Sign up for our eNe
 wsletter! Program generously sponsored by the Friends of the Worcester Pub
 lic Library\n\nSources: Discover Central Massachusetts\nEvent page: https:
 //www.discovercentralma.org/event/spotted-lanternfly-workshop/31164/
LOCATION:Worcester Public Library\, 3 Salem St.\, Worcester\, MA\, 01608
URL:https://www.discovercentralma.org/event/spotted-lanternfly-workshop/31
 164/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Suzanne Jackson in Conversation with Imani Perry
DTSTART:20261112T000000
DTEND:20261112T000000
DTSTAMP:20261110T190930Z
UID:f042ee106baeb8cc94ff0409d4f29e@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:Join artist Suzanne Jackson and scholar and writer Imani Perry
  for an exploration of Jackson’s six-decade career\, celebrated in the M
 FA exhibition “Suzanne Jackson: What Is Love.” From her ethereal early
  canvases to recent abstractions that suspend paint in midair\, Jackson ha
 s continually reinvented her practice while remaining rooted in memory\, p
 lace\, and material exploration. In conversation with Perry\, whose interd
 isciplinary scholarship focuses on the history of Black thought\, art\, an
 d imagination\, Jackson discusses the experiences and environments that ha
 ve shaped her life and work. Suzanne Jackson\, artist Imani Perry\, Henry 
 A. Morss Jr. And Elisabeth W. Morss Professor of Studies of Women\, Gender
 \, and Sexuality and of African and African American Studies\, and Carol K
 . Pforzheimer Professor\, Harvard Radcliffe Institute LEARN MORE / REGISTE
 R\n\nSources: Meet Boston\nEvent page: https://www.meetboston.com/event/su
 zanne-jackson-in-conversation-with-imani-perry/93961/
LOCATION:Museum of Fine Arts\, Boston\, Museum of Fine Arts\, Boston 465 H
 untington Avenue\, Boston\, MA\, 02115
URL:https://www.meetboston.com/event/suzanne-jackson-in-conversation-with-
 imani-perry/93961/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The American Revolution on Trial
DTSTART:20261112T000000
DTEND:20261112T000000
DTSTAMP:20261110T190930Z
UID:da7d9aa905c44fdab31b04ac22b8cd@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:In this virtual talk\, T.H. Breen discusses his new book\, The
  American Revolution on Trial: A New Nation Confronts the Burden of Indepe
 ndence (March 2026)\, which reconstructs a strange court martial held in C
 ambridge\, Massachusetts in 1778. General John Burgoyne\, himself then a p
 risoner of war\, demanded that leading Continental officers try an honored
  American colonel for possible abuse of British soldiers. The trial was on
 ly marginally a military story. Rather\, it raised troublesome issues abou
 t achieving cultural independence from Great Britain--even during a war--a
 nd demonstrated how preserving the rule of law was important for people ea
 ger to establish an honorable reputation for the New Republic. Register fo
 r this virtual program here. T.H. Breen has written several prize-winning 
 books on the American Revolution\, including Marketplace of Revolution: Ho
 w Consumer Politics Shaped American Independence (2004)\, which won the Co
 lonial War Society Prize for the best book in 2004 on the American Revolut
 ion . Breen’s research and writing explore how the experiences of ordina
 ry Americans were as important as the actions of the Founding Fathers in m
 obilizing revolutionary resistance and achieving national independence. Br
 een has taught at Northwestern University\, the Universities of Oxford and
  Cambridge\, and at the California Institute of Technology. He was elected
  to AAS membership in April 1994.\n\nSources: Discover Central Massachuset
 ts\nEvent page: https://www.discovercentralma.org/event/the-american-revol
 ution-on-trial/30658/
LOCATION:American Antiquarian Society\, 185 Salisbury St.\, Worcester\, MA
 \, 01609
URL:https://www.discovercentralma.org/event/the-american-revolution-on-tri
 al/30658/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Art of Romance
DTSTART:20261112T000000
DTEND:20261112T000000
DTSTAMP:20261110T190930Z
UID:6430bab5cd7be7ee556710248a2ff7@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:An evening of art and conversation celebrating Elaine Gignilli
 at’s remarkable romance cover paintings. What makes a painting tell a st
 ory? How does an artist capture desire\, anticipation\, danger\, or the sh
 ifting balance of power between two people in a single image? And what do 
 we discover when we look beyond the book cover to the painting itself? Joi
 n us for an evening of art and conversation celebrating Elaine Gignilliat
 \, whose original paintings helped shape the visual language of historical
  romance during the 1970s\, '80s\, and '90s. Featuring new additions to th
 e exhibition alongside paintings already on view\, the evening offers a ch
 ance to experience these remarkable works at full scale and discover the e
 xtraordinary artistry behind their familiar paperback covers. LEARN MORE /
  GET TICKETS\n\nSources: Meet Boston\nEvent page: https://www.meetboston.c
 om/event/the-art-of-romance/94190/
LOCATION:Lovestruck Books\, Lovestruck Books 44 Brattle Street\, Cambridge
 \, MA\, 02138
URL:https://www.meetboston.com/event/the-art-of-romance/94190/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Beauty Queen of Leenane
DTSTART:20261112T000000
DTEND:20261122T000000
DTSTAMP:20261110T190930Z
UID:5a6466e8b2effdaa9a5db1858e7dd8@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:Set in Rural Ireland\, this riveting Drama Desk and Tony Award
  winning Dark Comedy centers around a manipulative mother and her spinster
  daughter who are trapped in a cycle of dependence and fear that will not 
 only leave audiences laughing but also gasping in horror at the sinister u
 ndercurrent that Martin McDonagh has so brilliantly woven into his writing
 . The New York Times (Ben Brantley) praised the production as “a gasp fo
 r breath inside an airless life”\, highlighting the devastating and clau
 strophobic intensity of the mother-daughter power struggle. Come see Norto
 n Award winning Theater UnCorked as it brings you this brilliant and bruta
 l dark comedy. MORE INFO / GET TICKETS\n\nSources: Meet Boston\nEvent page
 : https://www.meetboston.com/event/the-beauty-queen-of-leenane/93674/
LOCATION:BCA Plaza Theatre\, BCA Plaza Theatre 539 Tremont Street\, Boston
 \, MA
URL:https://www.meetboston.com/event/the-beauty-queen-of-leenane/93674/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Hundred Aker Wood
DTSTART:20261112T000000
DTEND:20261213T000000
DTSTAMP:20261110T190930Z
UID:512a847ead30b744baacc0c7dd9415@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:Cape Rep Theatre continues our 2026 season with the world prem
 iere musical The Hundred Aker Wood\, written by Macklin Devine\, Seamus De
 vine\, Paddo Devine and Maura Hanlon. The show is directed by Maura Hanlon
 . Performances will be held in Cape Rep’s Indoor Theater November 12 - D
 ecember 13\, Wednesday - Saturday at 7:30 pm\, Sunday at 2 pm\; additional
  matinees Friday 11/27\, Saturday 11/14\, 11/21\, 11/28\, 12/05 and 12/12 
 at 2:00 pm. Tickets are $45\, $30 for 35 and under\; $25 for 18 and under.
  Student Rush 1/2 price at the door day of show and Pay-What-You-Like the 
 first Friday 11/13. Don’t miss Cape Cod Chronicle Night in the Barn on T
 hursday\, November 12\, 6:30 pm. Call the box office or see us online at c
 aperep.org for reservations and information. Cape Rep Indoor Theater\, Nor
 th Side Route 6A E. Brewster\, 508.896.1888 or www.caperep.org. The Hundre
 d Aker Wood Written by Macklin Devine\, Seamus Devine\, Paddo Devine and M
 aura Hanlon Directed by Maura Hanlon Musical Direction by Scott Storr Set 
 Design by Ryan McGettigan Costume Design by Robin McLaughlin Lighting Desi
 gn by Susan Nicholson Stage Manager Brielle Whalen We are delighted to con
 tinue our mission of supporting new works by local artists with the world 
 premiere musical\, The Hundred Aker Wood by Macklin Devine\, Paddo Devine
 \, Seamus Devine and Maura Hanlon. This production embodies what we value 
 most at Cape Rep: giving the artists in our community the opportunity to c
 reate\, collaborate\, take artistic risks\, and share new stories with our
  audiences. The Hundred Aker Wood is the second musical created by this cr
 eative team\, following their work Archibald Avery. Like so much of the wo
 rk we champion at Cape Rep\, it is a story sparked by the imaginations of 
 artists right here in our community and brought to life through collaborat
 ion. When A.A. Milne wrote Winnie-the-Pooh and The House at Pooh Corner \,
  he wrote them for everyone. In this adaptation of the stories that became
  classics\, it too\, is meant for everyone at any age. It’s a perfect wa
 y to bring in the holiday season. It’s nearly 11 and Billy Moon has an a
 ppointment. It’s his new aide Virgil’s first day and he doesn’t want
  to be late\, but Billy would rather kill some time than be early for anot
 her appointment in a life that has been reduced to nothing but appointment
 s. Who knew killing time could be so much fun? The Hundred Aker Wood is a 
 story told with humor\, heart and wonderful new songs. Directed by Maura H
 anlon\, with musical direction by Scott Storr\, the production features sc
 enic design by Ryan McGettigan\, costumes by Robin McLaughlin\, lighting b
 y Susan Nicholson\, and stage management by Brielle Whalen. And then there
 ’s the cast—a marvelous gathering of Cape Rep favorites\, including Ma
 cklin Devine\, Art Devine\, Zack Johnson\, Christina Morrell\, Trish LaRos
 e\, Ian Hamilton\, and Brian Lore Evans. With this much talent in one plac
 e\, the stage is sure to be buzzing with energy\, mischief\, and a little 
 bit of magic. All performances will be in Cape Rep’s Indoor Theater\, 32
 99 Main Street\, Brewster. New this season\, more matinees! You asked\, we
  listened! We have added more matinees across the season. Check each show
 ’s schedule for exact dates! For further information or to purchase tick
 ets\, contact the box office at 508.896.1888 or online at www.caperep.org.
 \n\nSources: Cape Cod Chamber\nEvent page: https://www.capecodchamber.org/
 event/the-hundred-aker-wood/52617/
LOCATION:Cape Rep Theatre\, 3299 Route 6A PO Box 1305\, East Brewster\, MA
 \, 02631
URL:https://www.capecodchamber.org/event/the-hundred-aker-wood/52617/
END:VEVENT
