BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Chorus pro Musica: Anthracite Fields
DTSTART:20261114T000000
DTEND:20261114T000000
DTSTAMP:20261110T190930Z
UID:ebff444c0ae0a7f8ea680bbed625b8@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:Chorus pro Musica presents the Boston premiere of Julia Wolfe'
 s Pulitzer Prize-winning Anthracite Fields\, produced by Bang on a Can All
 -Stars and conducted by Jamie Kirsch. Julia Wolfe’s Pulitzer Prize-winni
 ng oratorio Anthracite Fields is a powerful tribute to the coal miners of 
 Pennsylvania and the communities built on their labor. Scored for orchestr
 a and chorus\, the work weaves together miners’ names\, oral histories\,
  advertisements\, and a child’s spelling lesson into a hypnotic\, deeply
  moving musical portrait of a vanished way of life — and the human cost 
 behind it. Equal parts documentary\, elegy\, and celebration\, Anthracite 
 Fields is a strikingly original addition to the American musical canon\, b
 lending minimalist orchestral writing with the raw power of the human voic
 e and the exceptional talents of the Bang on a Can All-Stars. LEARN MORE /
  GET TICKETS\n\nSources: Meet Boston\nEvent page: https://www.meetboston.c
 om/event/chorus-pro-musica%3a-anthracite-fields/93429/
LOCATION:Emerson Cutler Majestic Theatre\, Emerson Cutler Majestic Theatre
  219 Tremont Street\, Boston\, MA\, 02116
URL:https://www.meetboston.com/event/chorus-pro-musica%3a-anthracite-field
 s/93429/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Concert at “Swett’s Tavern”: Ampersand Presents Music of a N
 ew Nation
DTSTART:20261114T140000
DTEND:20261114T160000
DTSTAMP:20261110T190930Z
UID:a72990a01f53bd4dd56ab76640dc79@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:Enjoy Ampersand’s special American Revolution–era program
 \, Music of the New Nation\, set within the historic Swett-Illsley House\,
  once a bustling colonial tavern. For this special evening\, we will trans
 form the house into Swett’s Tavern\, featuring period-inspired pub snack
 s and beer.The immersive performance explores life on the home front and t
 he experiences of everyday people as they faced the struggle for independe
 nce. The program features songs reflecting the early stirrings of revoluti
 on\, the lives of colonists from varied walks of life\, and the voices of 
 soldiers on both sides of the conflict. Highlights include a fiddle tune c
 omposed by an enslaved person\, as well as protest songs written during th
 e Revolution. This engaging trio performs on guitar\, mandolin\, bouzouki
 \, hammered dulcimer\, cello\, and penny whistles\, weaving rich vocal har
 monies with a palpable joy in sharing this historic music with modern audi
 ences.\n\nSources: North of Boston\nEvent page: https://northofboston.org/
 event/concert-at-swetts-tavern-ampersand-presents-music-of-a-new-nation/
LOCATION:Swett-Illsley House\, 4 High Road\, Newbury\, MA\, 01951
URL:https://northofboston.org/event/concert-at-swetts-tavern-ampersand-pre
 sents-music-of-a-new-nation/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Continuum
DTSTART:20261114T000000
DTEND:20261115T000000
DTSTAMP:20261110T190930Z
UID:7d49ed5056039fb5fe541a61d84972@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:Continuum traces dance’s ongoing evolution—where the past 
 shapes the present and every movement opens a path forward. Featuring six 
 works never before seen in Boston\, the program explores memory\, change\,
  freedom\, and our shared humanity. Intimate\, honest\, and alive\, Contin
 uum reminds us that we are always becoming. Ali Cederholm (Cisne) is a New
  York–based artist and 2025 summa cum laude graduate of Boston Conservat
 ory at Berklee. Handpicked by John Lam for LDW’s inaugural season\, she 
 returns to create her first commissioned work for the company—a deeply p
 ersonal expression of her emerging voice. Chloe Crenshaw (Synthetica) is a
  New York–based choreographer whose work has been commissioned by Ballet
 Collective\, Whim W’Him\, and Houston Contemporary 2. A 2026 Springboard
  Danse Emerging Choreographer and former dancer with GALLIM and Ballet BC
 \, she brings a compelling new voice to contemporary dance. Jon Ole Olstad
  (Dust and Water) is a Norwegian choreographer\, educator\, and former Ned
 erlands Dans Theater 1 dancer. An award-winning dance maker known for phys
 ical intensity and emotional honesty\, he has taught and created work at J
 uilliard\, NYU Tisch\, the Ailey School\, and Alonzo King LINES Ballet. Ke
 n Ossola (Moving Mountains) is an internationally renowned choreographer a
 nd former Nederlands Dans Theater artist whose work is celebrated for its 
 beauty\, refinement\, and emotional depth. A trusted collaborator of John 
 Lam\, he returns to create his third work for LDW. Robert Stephen (re:conn
 ection) is an award-winning Canadian choreographer\, educator\, and former
  First Soloist with The National Ballet of Canada. A former classmate of J
 ohn Lam at Canada’s National Ballet School\, he creates his first commis
 sioned work for LDW. Yury Yanowsky (Bathers) is an award-winning choreogra
 pher and former Boston Ballet Principal Dancer whose work is bold\, athlet
 ic\, and emotionally charged. A longtime collaborator of John Lam\, he ret
 urns with his third work presented by LDW. LEARN MORE / GET TICKETS\n\nSou
 rces: Meet Boston\nEvent page: https://www.meetboston.com/event/continuum/
 94209/
LOCATION:Emerson Paramount Center\, Emerson Paramount Center 559 Washingto
 n Street\, Boston\, MA
URL:https://www.meetboston.com/event/continuum/94209/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Daily Guided & Self-Guided Tour of Hammond Castle Museum
DTSTART:20261114T090000
DTEND:20261114T153000
DTSTAMP:20261110T190930Z
UID:8fc9b623624397be47661fa853a3a7@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:Step inside the breathtaking home of one of the most prolific 
 inventors of the 20th century\, John Hays Hammond\, Jr. Hammond Castle Mus
 eum is a dynamic STEAM museum\,*blending its historical significance as bo
 th **a historic house museum and a lapidarium. Inside\, you’ll not only 
 discover ancient\, medieval\, and Renaissance architectural elements and a
 rtifacts—including stone archways\, sarcophagi\, grave markers\, and an 
 extraordinary collection of stone and marble works—but you’ll also fin
 d a vibrant museum offering fresh educational and cultural arts programmin
 g. Visitors can explore items from the archival collection that have not b
 een publicly seen in generations\, along with newly curated content and th
 e most up-to-date information on the provenance of many pieces in our coll
 ection. New this season\, the Museum features four temporary exhibits that
  explore John Hays Hammond Jr. as both an individual and a scientific inno
 vator. In the Natalie Hays Hammond Temporary Exhibit Room\, Curatorial Dir
 ector John Leysath presents Spinning Platters with Hammond: Inside the Wor
 ld of Vinyl Records\, on display from April 29th through August 31st. Begi
 nning September 10th and running through the end of the 2025 season on Jan
 uary 4th\, Leysath will showcase” Hammond in Italy: The Love Affair Betw
 een the Hammonds & Italian Culture.”Additionally\, the Inventions Exhibi
 t Room will highlight Hammond’s groundbreaking Videotape Technology duri
 ng the spring and summer months\, followed by Hammond’s ahead-of-their-t
 ime Advertising Signs in the fall and early winter. Listed on the National
  Register of Historic Places\, and a repeated Reader’s Choice Best of No
 rth Shore winner by Northshore Magazine and a Trip Advisor Traveler’s Ch
 oice award recipient for the last three consecutive years\,** you can trus
 t that your visit will be an unforgettable experience. New This Season: Ne
 w Daily Hours and Flexible Arrival Times for Self-Guided Tours Open daily 
 from 9:00 am to 3:30 pm\, with the last entrance at 2:45 pm. The Museum\, 
 Museum Shop\, and grounds close at 3:30 pm. Experience Hammond Castle Muse
 um at your own pace with our new flexible arrival times for self-guided to
 urs. Your ticket grants you entry anytime within designated periods\, ensu
 ring you can arrive stress-free and enjoy your visit to the fullest. Morni
 ng Session: 9:00 am – 11:45 am / Afternoon Session: 11:45 am – 2:30 pm
  Self-guided tours allow you to explore at your own pace. We recommend pla
 nning for at least 60 minutes to experience the Museum’s fascinating gal
 leries\, grounds\, and temporary exhibits. **Accessibility Note: Please be
  aware that at nearly a century old\, the Hammond Castle Museum is not han
 dicapped accessible due to many difficult and winding stairs on the campus
  and within the Museum\n\nSources: North of Boston\nEvent page: https://no
 rthofboston.org/event/daily-guided-self-guided-tour-of-hammond-castle-muse
 um/2026-11-14/
LOCATION:Hammond Castle Museum\, 80 Hesperus Avenue\, Gloucester\, MA\, 01
 930
URL:https://northofboston.org/event/daily-guided-self-guided-tour-of-hammo
 nd-castle-museum/2026-11-14/
END:VEVENT
