BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Particular Power of Y-DNA Testing with FGS President Tim Marti
 n
DTSTART:20261114T000000
DTEND:20261114T000000
DTSTAMP:20261110T190930Z
UID:ed652222fa4a391321cd5e84e8a4b0@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:The Falmouth Genealogical Society (FGS) will hold its monthly 
 talk Saturday\, November 14\, 2026\, from 10:00 am-12:00 pm at the Falmout
 h Public Library\, 300 Main Street\, Falmouth. This talk is free and open 
 to the public. It will be held in person and is also available via Zoom. F
 or the Zoom credentials\, please contact president@falgen.org by Friday\, 
 November 13\, 2026. For more information\, visit the FGS website at www.fa
 lgen.org. Speaking in person\, FGS Board President Tim Martin will present
 : The Particular Power of Y-DNA Testing. Unlike the more commonly tested a
 utosomal DNA\, Y-DNA is duplicated exactly from father to son\, except rou
 ghly every few generations a single “SNP” mutation occurs which is pas
 sed on to all subsequent male descendants\, thereby defining distinguishab
 le male lineage branches through all time. This purely father to son linea
 ge is referred to as the patrilineal line. This talk will focus on the par
 ticular power of Y-DNA testing to reach back in time and positively reveal
  patrilineal ancestors and their patrilineal descendants. Any two males ta
 king a Big-Y test can determine an estimate of time to a common patrilinea
 l ancestor. We examine the case of Laurens Andriessen\, a Danish immigrant
 \, who arrived in New Amsterdam (present day Manhattan\, New York) about 1
 654. Upon arrival\, he took on the surname “Van Buskirk” and married a
  Dutch widow who bore him four sons. Y-DNA testing among 28 participating 
 males and conventional genealogy for some of those tested has allowed us t
 o determine who is and who is not a descendent of Laurens\, as well as fro
 m which of the four sons many of the testers descend. Several paternal sur
 prises are also revealed. We also discuss the benefits of Y-DNA testers jo
 ining surname and haplo-groups to better collaborate with and learn from t
 heir Y-DNA matches. Tim Martin was born in Lahore\, Pakistan while his fat
 her was teaching physics at Forman Christian College — his mother clearl
 y also present. He grew up in the small-town Chicago suburb of Lisle\, Ill
 inois. During high school\, his father returned to teaching physics in Dha
 hran\, Saudi Arabia\, sending Tim to the Community School in Tehran\, Iran
 \, where he first met his wife-to-be\, Deb Spohr. From a young age he was 
 never bored\, being furnished with self-motivation spawned by great curios
 ity and a love for music and science. He notes that his family has been se
 riously religious and deeply inquisitive. While insisting on a balance of 
 music courses\, he received a Bachelor of Arts degree in physics from Belo
 it College in 1974. He then found an opportunity to work at the Argonne Na
 tional Laboratory in the Chicago area using acoustics in atmospheric scien
 ce field work to measure winds\, where he remained “never facing reality
 .” There Tim and Deb raised five children\, retiring to Cape Cod in 2014
  and moving to the former home of Deb’s parents in Mashpee. Tim likes to
  tout that his pedigree tree “for five generations\, including myself\, 
 consists of Southern Indiana farmers.” His descent into genealogy was in
 evitable. Between his mother's marvelous family history writings\, father
 ’s telling diaries\, and the looming legend of his 2x great-grandfather
 \, “Big Dave” Van Buskirk — famously known as the tallest man in the
  Union Army — Tim’s curiosity required the truth. Driven to separate f
 act from tall tales\, he became hooked on discovering the people behind th
 e stories. Tim still loves “good music” and working on the personal hi
 story of people — so we can better understand ourselves and each other. 
 In recent years\, he has paid dearly for many Big-Y DNA tests to better ex
 plore the history of several family names. In his talk\, he will share som
 e of that exciting detective work with you. No Registration Required\n\nSo
 urces: Cape Cod Chamber\nEvent page: https://www.capecodchamber.org/event/
 the-particular-power-of-y-dna-testing-with-fgs-president-tim-martin/52704/
LOCATION:Falmouth Public Library\, 300 Main Street\, Falmouth\, MA\, 02540
URL:https://www.capecodchamber.org/event/the-particular-power-of-y-dna-tes
 ting-with-fgs-president-tim-martin/52704/
END:VEVENT
