BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Newburyport Art presents May Exhibitions at 65 Water Street
DTSTART:20261205T110000
DTEND:20261205T170000
DTSTAMP:20261110T190930Z
UID:2e133fed7622b79d9a7e83b6842e93@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:Newburyport Art (NAA) presents three new shows opening in the 
 month of May! Soft Structures is a group exhibition that explores fibers a
 nd fibrous material as process and metaphor. This exhibit features over 80
  artworks in a variety of media including drawing\, painting\, sculpture\,
  weaving\, embroidery\, quilting\, collage\, mixed media and more. This ex
 hibit is on view in the first-floor Sargent Gallery and second-floor Harts
 on Gallery. FOLIA is a collaborative exhibition featuring works by Amesbur
 y artists Amy Vander Els & Heather Karp. This exhibit explores the quiet b
 eauty and joyful rhythm of leaves and the symbolic meaning behind many tha
 t speaks of perseverance\, resilience and hope. Thirty-six artworks are di
 splayed amongst spring greenery in the first-floor Hills Gallery. Amy and 
 Heather will give a free artist talk on Thursday\, May 21st from 6:00-7:00
 pm\, concluding with Q&A. The 11th Annual Outdoor Sculpture Garden Exhibit
 ion debuted on May 1st\, and will remain on view year-round until April 20
 27. At nearly 3\,000 square feet\, the Range Lights Sculpture Garden resid
 es behind the historic NAA and is seen and visited by hundreds of people e
 very week. This year’s exhibition features 16 pieces by 10 local and reg
 ional artists. The participating artists include\; Jeffrey Briggs\, Lindle
 y Briggs\, Sarah Burns and Mike Platt\, Alexis Guieb-Alimbuyuguen\, Andrew
  Henschel\, Mae Kenny\, Evan Ralph\, Michael Updike and Raynor Warner. A r
 eception for FOLIA and Soft Structures will be held on Saturday\, May 2nd 
 from 4-6pm\, with the 11th Annual Sculpture Exhibit reception beginning at
  3:00pm\, including a Best in Show award at 3:30pm. The receptions are fre
 e and open to the public\, and visitors participating in the May 2nd Newbu
 ryport Artwalk are encouraged to stop by. New Hours: Wednesday through Sun
 day from 11:00am–5:00pm. The gallery is handicap accessible. More inform
 ation\, as well as an online gallery is available at newburyportart.org. F
 ounded in 1948\, Newburyport Art\, a 501(c)(3) non-profit\, is dedicated t
 o promoting and exhibiting members’ work and to providing art education 
 and access to the visual arts for the entire community. The gallery and ev
 ents are free and open to the public.\n\nSources: North of Boston\nEvent p
 age: https://northofboston.org/event/newburyport-art-presents-may-exhibiti
 ons-at-65-water-street/2026-12-05/
LOCATION:Newburyport Art Association\, 65 Water St\, Newburyport\, MA\, 01
 950
URL:https://northofboston.org/event/newburyport-art-presents-may-exhibitio
 ns-at-65-water-street/2026-12-05/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Once Upon a Winter: Tales of Wonder Through a Child’s Eyes
DTSTART:20261205T160000
DTEND:20261205T170000
DTSTAMP:20261110T190930Z
UID:fb45c4770fe12c6ae36571e91b81ef@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:Cantemus Chamber Chorus\, with 42 members from across the Nort
 h Shore\, will present three concerts in December titled “Once Upon a Wi
 nter: Tales of Wonder Through a Child’s Eyes” in Newburyport\, Beverly
 \, and Gloucester. The concerts feature Matthew Harris’s “A Child’s 
 Christmas in Wales\,” based on the beloved holiday tale by Dylan Thomas.
  In addition\, the program will include a rich tapestry of contemporary an
 d traditional repertoire from different cultures reflecting themes of wond
 er\, home\, darkness\, returning light\, and memory. The repertoire includ
 es R. Murray Schafer’s “Snowforms\; “Song for Yalda Night\,” an Ir
 anian piece about the longest night of the year\; the “Kang Ding Love So
 ng\,” a Chinese folk song associated with the Winter Solstice\; Morten L
 auridsen’s “Lament for Pasiphaë\;” traditional Hanukkah songs “Mi
  Y’Maleil” and “S’vivon\;” Daniel Kantor’s “Night of Silence
 ” and more. In addition to accompanist Jeffrey Mead on piano\, joining C
 antemus will be an 11-piece chamber orchestra and the Lexington Children
 ’s Chorus (LCC)\, an auditioned choral ensemble for singers in grades 4-
 6. Founded by Artistic Director Allison Iannuzzi in 2019\, the LCC has col
 laborated with the Lexington Symphony Orchestra\, the Lexington High Schoo
 l Choral Program\, the Middlesex County Volunteer Fifes & Drums\, and othe
 r community arts partners. Cantemus’ concerts will take place in Newbury
 port on Saturday\, December 5 at St. Paul’s Church\, 166 High St.\; in B
 everly on Saturday\, December 12 at St. Peter’s Episcopal Church\, 4 Oce
 an St.\; and in Gloucester on Sunday\, December 13 at the Unitarian Univer
 salist Church\, 10 Church St. All three performances are at 4:00 p.m. Cant
 emus’ Artistic Director Jason Iannuzzi said: “Across cultures and cent
 uries\, the year’s longest nights have drawn people together to light ca
 ndles\, gather around tables\, tell stories\, and sing.“ Though customs 
 differ from place to place\, “they often spring from the same desires: f
 or warmth\, for home\, for hope\, and for one another. This concert traces
  this shared story through the eyes of a child.” Allison Iannuzzi said\,
  “There is something truly special about experiencing the wonder of the 
 winter season through a child’s eyes. The Children’s Chorus is delight
 ed to collaborate with Cantemus to share music celebrating the beauty of w
 inter traditions. We hope this music invites us all to rediscover the magi
 c of the season and the shared experiences that unite us.” For more info
 rmation about the program and to purchase tickets\, visit www.cantemus.org
 /tickets. Tickets may also be purchased at the door\, space permitting. Ca
 ntemus gratefully acknowledges sponsorship support from Eastern Bank\, Ins
 titution for Savings\, and Rockland Trust\, and funding support from the L
 ocal Cultural Councils of Newbury\, Newburyport\, Rockport\, and West Newb
 ury. Cantemus participates in the Card to Culture program\, a Mass Cultura
 l Council initiative that offers reduced-price tickets to card holders. Ca
 ntemus is a member of MASSCreative.org and the Greater Boston Choral Conso
 rtium.\n\nSources: North of Boston\nEvent page: https://northofboston.org/
 event/once-upon-a-winter-tales-of-wonder-through-a-childs-eyes/
LOCATION:St. Paul&#8217\;s Episcopal Church\, Newburyport\, 166 High St.\,
  Newburyport\, MA\, 01950
URL:https://northofboston.org/event/once-upon-a-winter-tales-of-wonder-thr
 ough-a-childs-eyes/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Origami Unfolded Opening Celebration
DTSTART:20261205T103000
DTEND:20261205T160000
DTSTAMP:20261110T190930Z
UID:4c880f3fab26bd21c2b1b4b991bb8f@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:Join us as we celebrate the opening of Origami Unfolded! Watch
  origami artists show off their skills during demonstrations\, touch folde
 d pieces at our discovery cart and learn to fold your own paper creations.
  Origami Unfolded investigates recent developments in origami art and orig
 ami-inspired breakthroughs in science\, engineering and math. Origami Herm
 it Crab: Beginner 10:30 am–11:30 am | Create Space Studio Included with 
 admission\; same-day preregistration required. This workshop with artist B
 eth Johnson guides you through creating a detailed origami shell and the c
 lever little hermit crab that calls it home. The geometric shell is surpri
 singly simple to fold\, yet incredibly satisfying\, revealing beautiful hi
 dden structures as you work. Whale on Water: Low–Intermediate 11:45 am
 –12:45 pm | Create Space Studio Included with admission\; same-day prere
 gistration required. This workshop with artist Beth Johnson combines class
 ic origami traditions with a brief introduction to the more abstract techn
 iques of wet folding and curved folding. Participants will fold a cute\, s
 tylized origami whale and an elegant wave base using wet and curved foldin
 g. Hedgehog\, Snail\, and Peacock: Intermediate 3–4 pm | Create Space St
 udio Included with admission\; same-day preregistration required. This wor
 kshop introduces participants to a highly versatile\, corrugated origami p
 attern. From a single geometric foundation\, you will learn how to sculpt 
 up to three animals. Origami Artist Demonstrations 11 am–1 pm\, 2–3 pm
  | Main Atrium Included with admission Watch origami in real time with mas
 ter artists whose work is featured in the exhibition! Lecture and Q&A with
  Bernie Peyton: Why Origami? 1–1:45 pm | Morse Auditorium Included with 
 admission\; same-day preregistration required. The principles of folding h
 ave been fundamental to the design of spacecraft\, fashion\, medical devic
 es\, packaging and architecture. Master origami artist Bernie Peyton explo
 res origami-inspired works and the sparks that led to their creation. Main
  Atrium\, Create Space Studios\, and Morse Auditorium All activities are i
 ncluded with admission. Some workshops require same-day preregistration.\n
 \nSources: North of Boston\nEvent page: https://northofboston.org/event/or
 igami-unfolded-opening-celebration/
LOCATION:Peabody Essex Museum\, East India Square\, 161 Essex Street\, Sal
 em\, MA\, 01970
URL:https://northofboston.org/event/origami-unfolded-opening-celebration/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Phil Vassar with Annie Brobst
DTSTART:20261205T200000
DTEND:20261205T220000
DTSTAMP:20261110T190930Z
UID:050b1074c0cf3e2986da43e05e6263@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:Phil Vassar’s life has always been steeped in music. Raised 
 in a home where his father sang and his two sisters spun everything from M
 otown to West Coast harmonies\, his upbringing was a constant melody. The 
 gospel and bluegrass influence of Lynchburg’s unique geography further s
 haped his sound\, while artists like Bruce Hornsby\, the Statler Brothers
 \, Billy Joel\, Elton John\, and Lionel Richie sculpted his melodic sensib
 ilities. A true fan of rock legends like Queen\, Boston\, and David Bowie
 \, Vassar’s eclectic roots would lay the foundation for his remarkable c
 areer. This December show will include Phil Vassar originals\, along with 
 some Holiday surprises. Annie Brobst New England Music Awards’ Overall A
 ct of the Year\, Female Performer of the Year\, Country Artist of the Year
 \, and 2-time Song of the Year winner\, Brobst’s unique songwriting rang
 es from traditional country to pop country. With influences like Miranda L
 ambert\, Ashley McBryde and Jason Isbell\; she adds a feminine touch to a 
 bro-country sound and a powerful authenticity to storytelling through song
 .\n\nSources: North of Boston\nEvent page: https://northofboston.org/event
 /phil-vassar-with-annie-brobst/
LOCATION:The Cut\, 177 Main Street\, Gloucester\, MA\, 01930
URL:https://northofboston.org/event/phil-vassar-with-annie-brobst/
END:VEVENT
